"Craters and the Ages of the Moon’s Surface | VirtualDemos",
"<a>Virtual Physics Demos</a>",
"Craters and the Ages of the Moon’s Surface",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> Make a crater: Meteor Crater, Tycho, Copernicus, Chicxulub, Mead, Orientale, Caloris, Hellas, a {#} m asteroid. Count craters: lunar highlands, Mare Tranquillitatis, Alphonsus floor, Copernicus floor, Tycho floor, four Martian surfaces and a Mystery surface. Cooling: Just formed, When the lunar maria erupted, Today.",
"The demo has three parts, chosen at the top of the picture: Make a crater, Count craters and Cooling. Each has a 3D view and a flat view.",
"<strong>Make a crater.</strong> Choose the target (Moon, Mercury, Venus, Earth or Mars), what the asteroid is made of, its diameter, its speed and the angle at which it comes in, then drop it. The crater’s size comes from a scaling law fitted to experiments and computer models of impacts (Collins, Melosh and Marcus {#}). Small craters are simple bowls; bigger ones collapse into a flat floor with a central peak, and the biggest have a ring of peaks inside. The shapes drawn are simplified; the sizes are the law’s. The model uses one law for bowls and another for flat floored craters, and they do not meet: just past the change (about {#} km across on the Moon) a slightly bigger asteroid gives a crater about {#} % smaller. That step is a seam in the model, not something real craters do.",
"<strong>Count craters.</strong> Each surface is a simulated patch of the Moon or Mars, built crater by crater over its age. Click each crater at least as wide as the purple circle, with its center inside the square, to mark it. The graph is the crater clock: how many craters a surface collects as it ages, measured on the Moon by matching crater counts to the ages of rocks the Apollo astronauts brought back (Neukum, Ivanov and Hartmann {#}).",
"<strong>Cooling.</strong> A Moon, a Mars and an Earth start equally hot, made of the same rock with the same radioactive elements. The slider moves through the {#} billion years since they formed. The cold lid is the stiff outer layer that heat has to conduct through.",
"Record reading stores what the readouts show (in Cooling, one row for each planet). Export to worksheet sends the readings to the matching table below; Clear starts a new series.",
"See how an asteroid’s size sets the size and shape of its crater, use crater counts to put ages on the Moon’s surfaces, and find out why the small Moon went quiet long before Earth.",
"<strong>Craters.</strong> An asteroid hits at many kilometers per second, so it carries an enormous energy for its size. It blasts out a crater far wider than itself, typically {#} to {#} times its own diameter. In weak gravity (the Moon) the same asteroid makes a bigger crater than in strong gravity (Earth).",
"<strong>The crater clock.</strong> Asteroids keep falling, so a surface collects craters as it ages: the older the surface, the more craters it has. Counting the craters on a patch of ground, and dividing by its area, gives the number of craters per square kilometer. The Apollo and Luna missions brought back rocks whose ages are known from radioactive dating, from places whose craters had been counted; together they calibrate the clock. The clock is not a straight line: billions of years ago impacts were far more frequent.",
"<strong>Saturation.</strong> When a surface is very old, new craters land on top of old ones and wipe them out. The count then stops growing as fast as the clock says, and the age read from it comes out too young.",
"<strong>Cooling.</strong> A planet stores heat in its volume but loses it through its surface. A small planet has much more surface for each cubic kilometer of rock, so it loses its heat faster. As the inside cools, the cold outer lid thickens, and lava finds it harder to reach the surface.",
"An asteroid {#} km across makes a crater about {#} km wide. Make a guess: how wide a crater does an asteroid {#} km across make? Exactly {#} times wider, more than {#} times, or less?",
"Surface A has {#} craters wider than {#} km on a patch of {#} km². Surface B has {#} on a patch of {#} km². Which surface is older? How many craters per km² does each have?",
"A ball of radius R has surface area {#}πR² and volume ({#}/{#})πR³. Divide the area by the volume. The Moon’s radius is about one quarter of Earth’s: how many times more surface does the Moon have for each cubic kilometer?",
"<strong>Part {#}: craters on the Moon.</strong> In Make a crater, choose the Moon as the target and a dense rock asteroid at {#} km/s coming in at {#}°. Drop asteroids {#}, {#}, {#}, {#}, {#}, {#} and {#} km across, and record each crater once it has formed. Then record the Chicxulub setup, and finally a {#} km dense rock asteroid at {#} km/s and {#}° hitting Earth. Send the readings to the worksheet, then clear them.",
"<strong>Part {#}: dating the Moon’s surfaces.</strong> In Count craters, choose each of these five surfaces in turn: Mare Tranquillitatis, Highlands, Alphonsus floor, Copernicus floor and Tycho floor. Keep the size to count that the setup chooses. Mark every crater at least as wide as the purple circle whose center lies inside the square, then record a reading. Send all five readings to the worksheet, then clear them.",
"<strong>Part {#}: three planets cooling.</strong> In Cooling, choose When the lunar maria erupted ({#} billion years after the planets formed) and record a reading, then choose Today and record another. Send them to the worksheet.",
